{
  "raw": {
    "https://tre.tbe.taleo.net/tre01/ats/careers/v2/viewRequisition?org=OCADU&cws=37&rid=3861": {
      "title": "Curriculum Specialist, Indigenous Learning",
      "description": "Department: Centre for Learning and Teaching\nCurriculum Specialist, Indigenous Learning | Job Code | 3861 | Department | Centre for Learning and Teaching | Employment Type | Temporary/Contract\nNew Search Login Page Position Description Curriculum Specialist, Indigenous Learning Job Code 3861 Department Centre for Learning and Teaching Employment Type Temporary/Contract Apply Now OCAD University acknowledges the ancestral territories of the Mississaugas of the Credit, the Haudenosaunee, the Anishinaabeg and the Huron-Wendat, who are the original owners and custodians of the land on which we live, work and create. OCAD University is Canada’s largest and oldest art and design university, internationally recognized for creativity, innovation, and interdisciplinary learning. As the University advances its Academic and Strategic Plan, we are continuing to stengthen our commitment to Indigenous learning, decolonization, and meaningful institutional change. The Opportunity OCAD University is seeking a Curriculum Specialist, Indigenous Learning to lead strategic initiatives that advance Indigenous teaching, learning, and student success across the University. Working within the Centre for Learning & Teaching (CLT) and reporting to the Manager, Teaching Support & Curriculum Enhancement, the Curriculum Specialist collaborates closely with the Director, Indigenous Initiatives, and a broad network of Indigenous and academic partners to strengthen Indigenous curriculum development and renewal across undergraduate and graduate programs. Through curriculum review, faculty engagement, online course development, and the renewal of OCAD U's Indigenous Learning Outcomes (ILOs), the successful candidate will help advance Indigenous histories, perspectives, knowledges, methodologies, and cultural art and design practices throughout the curriculum. As a member of CLT, the Curriculum Specialist will join a collaborative team of teaching and learning professionals dedicated to supporting faculty, students, Teaching Assistants, and instructional staff. Working in consultation with the Chair of the Indigenous Visual Culture (INVC) program, the Indigenous Student Centre (ISC), the Indigenous Education Council (IEC), and other campus and community partners, the successful candidate will play a key role in supporting OCAD U's commitment to Indigenous teaching and learning and the priorities of the University's Academic and Strategic Plan . What You’ll Do Advance Indigenous Curriculum and Student Success Lead and support curriculum development and renewal across OCAD U’s undergraduate and graduate programs, strengthening engagement with Indigenous histories, perspectives, knowledges, methodologies, and cultural practices in art and design Collaborate with the Indigenous Student Centre and Indigenous Visual Culture program to assess Indigenous students’ learning experiences and identify and address systemic barriers to learning Develop teaching and learning resources and faculty professional learning initiatives that support Indigenous student experiences and success Provide subject matter expertise in Indigenous pedagogies, methodologies, and decolonizing approaches to strengthen the Centre for Learning & Teaching’s programming and engagement Lead the Renewal of Indigenous Learning Outcomes (ILO) Organize and lead the redesign and renewal of OCAD U’s ILO Develop strategies, resources, documentation, and processes that support ongoing engagement with the ILO across the University Coordinate and conduct consultations with faculty, the Indigenous Education Council, the Indigenous Cultural Advisory Circle, and Indigenous community partners, including Six Nations of the Grand River and the Mississaugas of the Credit First Nation Develop Learning and Community-Engagement Initiatives Co-facilitate and support the design, development, and production of an online Indigenous art, design, and history course in collaboration with a multidisciplinary course-development team Develop and deliver community-engagement programming that strengthens faculty and staff understanding of Indigenous learning and decolonization Facilitate professional learning and complex conversations to build institutional capacity and support meaningful organizational change What You Bring Indigenous Citizenship, Knowledge and Community Relationships Indigenous citizenship (First Nations, Inuit, Métis), connection to an Indigenous community, a history and/or evidence of lived experience and an understanding of Indigenous culture(s) and practices In depth knowledge of Indigenous pedagogies, methodologies and ways of knowing at the post-secondary level, including decolonizing and Indigenizing pedagogies, land-based learning, storytelling, experiential learning, and intergenerational and relational approaches to teaching and learning Significant experience living and/or working with Indigenous communities and well-versed in protocol matters related to Indigenous relationships is essential Demonstrated intercultural competence; experience working with diverse Indigenous communities, including Elders, Knowledge Keepers, community members, faculty, academic administrators, administrative staff, program staff and students Understanding of the barriers and learning needs of Indigenous post-secondary students Education and Experience Successful completion of a graduate/Master's degree with a specialization in Indigenous Education, Curriculum Studies or similar, Ph.D./Ed.D preferred Four (4) years of progressive experience working in Indigenous educational programming, community engagement, or related fields, or an equivalent combination of education, community-based knowledge, and lived experience. Three (3) years of experience developing collaborative partnerships to advance decolonization, including experience of organizational change-based projects in comparable sectors; demonstrated experience facilitating professional learning to build institutional capacity Familiarity with post-secondary studies in art and design is essential; background in studio art or design a strong asset Post-secondary teaching experience or experience in a post-secondary teaching centre, writing centre or similar academic support unit is an asset. An equivalent combination of teaching, community, and/or professional experience demonstrating the required knowledge and competencies will also be considered. Curriculum, Consultation and Program Leadership Experience in the development and delivery of hybrid and online curriculum, an asset Demonstrated ability to manage programming with proven vision and initiative grounded in Indigenous epistemologies Program planning skills with ability to meet deadlines and manage complex projects Experience designing and implementing consultation and needs assessment processes Collaboration and Communication Facilitation and consensus-building skills, with demonstrated ability to navigate complex and sensitive conversations across diverse community members, with faculty and staff Strong collaborative work orientation; ability to work both independently and consultatively within a team environment Exceptional interpersonal and communication skills, with demonstrated ability to build collaborative relationships, anticipate and respond to resistance and influence curriculum development and educational initiatives through dialogue, consultation and partnership Excellent oral and written communication skills including academic and professional writing experience Commitment and Flexibility Demonstrated commitment to the principles and practices of equity, anti-racism, decolonizing and accessibility; experience promoting a respectful work and learning environment for students, faculty and staff Flexibility to adjust hours and location of work as needed to support programs and events outside of regular office hours and location Work Arrangement & Compensation Compensation: Hiring range: $46.33 to $53.11 per hour. Salary range: $46.33 to $60.88 per hour Hours of Work: Monday to Friday, for a total of 35 hours per week. This is a temporary contract expected to continue until May 31, 2027. Mode of Work: This position is eligible for hybrid work arrangements; however, a minimum of three (3) days per week of on campus work is required with flexibility to adjust the days and times of work on campus to meet programming needs. The review of applications has begun and will continue until the position is filled. Why Work at OCAD University A vibrant and creative work environment surrounded by artists, designers, educators, and researchers Access to employee assistance program Paid time off during the annual Winter Closure A centrally located downtown campus with easy access to public transit The opportunity to work collaboratively with Indigenous faculty, staff, students, leaders, and community partners A meaningful role supporting Indigenous student success and advancing curricular and institutional change If you are an Indigenous applicant and require support during the recruitment process, please reach out to Olivia Bowes, Talent Acquisition Coordinator at oliviabowes@ocadu.ca . Back Share Apply Now"
    },
    "https://tre.tbe.taleo.net/tre01/ats/careers/v2/viewRequisition?org=OCADU&cws=37&rid=3899": {
      "title": "Director, Strategic Communications",
      "description": "Department: Marketing & Communications\nDirector, Strategic Communications | Job Code | 3899 | Department | Marketing & Communications | Employment Type | Temporary/Contract\nNew Search Login Page Position Description Director, Strategic Communications Job Code 3899 Department Marketing & Communications Employment Type Temporary/Contract Apply Now OCAD University acknowledges the ancestral territories of the Mississaugas of the Credit, the Haudenosaunee, the Anishinaabeg and the Huron-Wendat, who are the original owners and custodians of the land on which we live, work and create OCAD University , Canada’s largest and oldest art and design university, is a vibrant community of bold, curious and compassionate artists, designers and scholars who are imagining and creating a joyful, equitable and sustainable world. The University is an internationally renowned hub for art, design, digital media, research, innovation and creativity, and arts administration. It embraces collaborative and interdisciplinary approaches to change-making through art, design-thinking curriculum and research, making OCAD U a local, regional, national and global leader in art and design. It is an exciting time to join the OCAD U community as the University is at a key stage in its institutional transformation, with the implementation of its Academic and Strategic Plan and guiding principles — driving positive impact; decolonization, indigenization and equity; environmental sustainability; emerging as a vibrant hub; student centered approaches; innovation in learning teaching and research; and financial stewardship. The Director, Strategic Communications leads OCAD University's (OCAD U) strategic communications function, developing and delivering integrated communications strategies that strengthen the University's brand, build public understanding and advance institutional priorities. Reporting to the Executive Director, Marketing & Communications, and working closely with the Director, Institutional Communications, the Director leads the University's proactive and strategic executive communications, internal communications, public relations, issues management, storytelling and content strategy. The successful candidate is first and foremost an exceptional communications leader. They recognize that communications practice has fundamentally evolved, and understand that communications, marketing and audience strategy are no longer separate disciplines, but complementary tools used to build awareness, trust, engagement and institutional value. They bring an audience-first mindset, balancing strategic insight with creativity, sound judgment and operational excellence. The successful candidate understands that the role of communications is no longer simply to inform. It is to influence understanding, earn attention and build long-term institutional value. Summary of Responsibilities: Strategic Communications Leadership Work with the Director, Institutional Communications to lead and develop a high-performing communications team, fostering creativity, collaboration, accountability and continuous improvement. Develop and execute integrated communications strategies that enhance OCAD University's reputation, strengthen its brand and advance institutional priorities. Translate institutional priorities into communications strategies that create measurable impact across multiple audiences. Establish priorities, allocate resources and ensure the successful delivery of multiple concurrent initiatives. Measure communications effectiveness and continuously refine strategies using research, audience insights and analytics. Brand, Narrative & Audience Strategy Lead the development and stewardship of OCAD University's narrative, ensuring communications consistently reinforce the University's distinctive brand. Translate complex academic, financial and institutional priorities into clear, compelling and audience-centred stories. Develop communications strategies informed by audience insight, research and analytics, ensuring messages resonate with different target audiences. Ensure every significant communication contributes to strengthening how OCAD University is understood, experienced and valued. Integrated Communications Lead the planning and execution of media relations, executive communications, internal communications, issues management and digital content. Develop integrated communications plans that complement institutional marketing strategies and reinforce a consistent institutional voice across earned, owned, shared and paid channels. Identify opportunities to amplify stories, campaigns and institutional priorities through coordinated communications and marketing efforts. Ensure communications are timely, relevant, audience-focused and aligned with institutional objectives. Executive Communications & Strategic Counsel Serve as a trusted communications advisor to the President, Executive Director and senior leadership. Develop speeches, presentations, briefing materials, key messages and executive communications, particularly focusing on change management. Provide strategic counsel on the communications implications of institutional decisions and emerging issues. Support senior leaders in building credibility and influence across higher education, the creative sector and public life. Reputation & Issues Management Anticipate reputational opportunities and risks and develop proactive communications strategies. Lead communications planning during complex, high-profile and sensitive issues. Develop messaging frameworks, stakeholder communications and executive briefing materials during periods of organizational change or crisis. Protect and strengthen the University's reputation through thoughtful, timely and audience-centred communications. Storytelling & Content Leadership Champion exceptional storytelling that reflects OCAD University's creativity, impact and distinctive character. Foster a culture of curiosity, experimentation and continuous improvement within the communications team. Identify stories that deepen audience engagement and reinforce institutional priorities. Ensure content is compelling, accessible and optimized for the platforms and audiences it serves. Qualifications: Undergraduate degree in Communications, Journalism, Public Relations, Marketing or a related discipline. Minimum of five (5) years of progressively senior communications leadership experience. Demonstrated success leading strategic communications within a complex organization – agencies, cultural institutions, government, purpose-driven organizations, media organizations and the private sector who bring a contemporary perspective on communications and audience engagement Exceptional experience in executive communications, media relations, issues management and organizational storytelling. Experience leading and developing high-performing teams. Demonstrated ability to advise senior executives and influence organizational decision-making. Experience developing integrated communications strategies informed by audience insight, brand thinking and marketing principles. Demonstrated experience building awareness and engagement through earned, owned, shared and paid communications. Knowledge, Skills & Competencies: The successful candidate will demonstrate: Exceptional strategic thinking and judgment. Outstanding writing, editing and storytelling abilities. Deep understanding of contemporary communications practice, including the integration of communications, marketing, audience strategy, brand and digital engagement to achieve organizational objectives. Demonstrated ability to translate complex ideas into clear, compelling and audience-centred communications. Strong understanding of contemporary media, digital platforms, audience behaviour and content strategy. Political acuity and the ability to navigate complex stakeholder environments with confidence and diplomacy. Strong leadership, coaching and relationship-building skills. Excellent project management and organizational abilities. Exercises excellent judgment in the use of AI as an integral part of professional communications practice, leveraging it to improve research, synthesis, planning, content development and decision-making while maintaining the highest standards of accuracy, originality and editorial quality. Demonstrated commitment to the principles of equity and diversity, and proven ability to deal effectively with a diverse population Mode of Work: Hybrid Compensation: $113,519.00 to $133,527.00 per year - commensurate with experience, plus 4% vacation pay and 7% in lieu of *benefits * The successful incumbent has the option of pay in lieu or enrollment in benefits. Hours of Work: Monday to Friday, 35 hours per week. This is a temporary contract with an expected duration of 1 year with possibility of renewal Application Deadline: Interested applicants are invited to submit an updated * resume and cover letter ( PDF ) by selecting \"Apply Now\" below. The review of applications will begin Thursday October 22 nd and will continue until the position has been filled. * Please upload your resume and cover letter as one PDF . Please name your upload: FirstName_LastName-Job Code As an institution committed to art, design, digital media and related scholarship, OCAD University recognizes the profound and essential value that diversity brings to the creation, reception and circulation of creative practices and discourse. OCAD University understands that valuing diverse creative practices and forms of knowledge are essential to and enrich the institution’s core mission and vision as an art and design university with a local and global scope. As an employer committed to employment equity, we encourage applications from members of equity-deserving communities including women, racialized and Indigenous persons, persons with disabilities, and persons of all sexual orientations and gender identities/expressions. OCAD University is committed to providing an inclusive and barrier free experience to applicants with accessibility needs. Requests for accommodation can be made at any stage during the recruitment process. Please contact People & Culture for more information or refer to OCADU’s Accommodation in Employment Policy . All qualified persons are encouraged to apply; however, Canadians and permanent residents of Canada will be given priority. While we thank all candidates for their interest, only those short-listed will be contacted. Back Share Apply Now"
    },
    "https://tre.tbe.taleo.net/tre01/ats/careers/v2/viewRequisition?org=OCADU&cws=37&rid=3895": {
      "title": "Employee Wellness Specialist",
      "description": "Department: People & Culture\nEmployee Wellness Specialist | Job Code | 3895 | Department | People & Culture | Employment Type | Temporary/Contract\nNew Search Login Page Position Description Employee Wellness Specialist Job Code 3895 Department People & Culture Employment Type Temporary/Contract Apply Now OCAD University acknowledges the ancestral territories of the Mississaugas of the Credit, the Haudenosaunee, the Anishinaabeg and the Huron-Wendat, who are the original owners and custodians of the land on which we live, work and create OCAD University , Canada’s largest and oldest art and design university, is a vibrant community of bold, curious and compassionate artists, designers and scholars who are imagining and creating a joyful, equitable and sustainable world. The University is an internationally renowned hub for art, design, digital media, research, innovation and creativity, and arts administration. It embraces collaborative and interdisciplinary approaches to change-making through art, design-thinking curriculum and research, making OCAD U a local, regional, national and global leader in art and design. It is an exciting time to join the OCAD U community as the University is at a key stage in its institutional transformation, with the implementation of its Academic and Strategic Plan and guiding principles — driving positive impact; decolonization, indigenization and equity; environmental sustainability; emerging as a vibrant hub; student centered approaches; innovation in learning teaching and research; and financial stewardship. Reporting to the Manager, People Strategies, the Employee Wellness Specialist is responsible for organization-wide programs focused on employee wellbeing, leaves of absence, human rights-based accommodation and return-to-work in keeping with OCAD University’s (OCAD U) strategic and academic plans and values. The Specialist provides advice and guidance to managers, employees, and others within the People & Culture office, and develops organizational policies and strategies in consultation with university stakeholders. This role also supports the implementation of formal processes and procedures to support the success of all OCAD U employees, with specific regard for the success of equity-deserving groups. Summary of Responsibilities: Leave, Accommodation & Disability Case Management In concert with managers and academic leaders, administer medical leaves of absence for all employee groups, including collecting required medical documentation, monitoring leave timelines, preparing documentation for benefits provider(s) and coordinating return to work arrangements, as applicable Provide end-to-end case management for leave, disability and accommodation matters, from intake and assessment through implementation, monitoring, return to work and case closure, ensuring timely communication, appropriate documentation and consistent application of university requirements. Manage complex disability management cases from onset to return to work; identifying opportunities for early intervention and safe, sustainable return to work strategies.  Administer the full range of statutory, contractual, medical and University-approved leaves of absence for all employee groups, applying relevant legislation, collective agreements, employment terms and University policies consistently. Maintain awareness of client group needs and support the P&C Advisor with workforce planning while supporting leave management. Coordinate and provide guidance on employee and applicant accommodation requests relating to disability and other protected grounds under the Ontario Human Rights Code, in accordance with applicable legislation and University policy. Facilitate the development of individualized accommodation plans based on an understanding of functional limitations and operational needs; implement, evaluate and modify accommodations as appropriate to support with successful re-boarding of employees. Research and assess accommodation and return-to-work options; provide evidence-informed recommendations to managers and academic leaders; document case decisions and rationales; and escalate complex, disputed or high-risk matters as appropriate. Negotiate and act on sensitive and complex issues pertaining to employee accommodations, initiating consultations and case conferences where necessary Maintain complete, accurate and secure leave and accommodation records, protecting medical and personal information and limiting disclosure to information reasonably required to administer the leave or implement the accommodation. In accordance with the university’s Accommodation in Employment Policy, coordinate applicant and employee accommodation needs; liaise with the Human Rights & Equity Office and Safety, Security & Campus Operations where appropriate Employee Wellbeing Research best practices, trends and new initiatives in employee wellness to inform and improve current programs Support in-house training initiatives related to wellness, human rights and equity, including assisting with development of annual training plan and facilitating information sessions, as required Participate as management-designated representative on the Joint Health & Safety Committee, as needed Develop and implement related policies, tools and training Evaluate, measure, and report on the effectiveness of wellness programs; make recommendations for changes Provide high-level customer service by ensuring seamless coordination of work with other People & Culture staff Manage related budget(s) and make recommendations for effective allocation of funds Assist the Manager, People Strategies in related functions and projects contributing to the successful operation of the People & Culture office Research & Policy Support Prepare internal and external reports as required; liaise with other departments and P&C staff to coordinate and collect related employee leave related data Maintain and analyze leave and accommodation case data to monitor service delivery, identify systemic trends and barriers, support workforce planning, and recommend improvements to policies, processes and preventative strategies. Conduct various cyclical audits to gain insights and establish needs as it relates to leave and accommodation management across the university Maintain an accurate electronic filing system as it relates to leaves and accommodation, and perform related tasks to ensure records are easily accessible to appropriate staff and kept in accordance with guidelines Conduct employee research and analysis in leave and accommodation best practices across the post-secondary sector. Using the content management system, review and update P&C section of the OCAD U website and respective SharePoint site, ensuring compliance with AODA standards Serve as a backup for other administrative and coordination functions within the People & Culture team, as needed Qualifications: A post-secondary degree in Human Resources, Business or a related field, or an equivalent combination of education and experience with special consideration for the professional and community-based experience of equity-deserving groups A Certified Human Resources Leader (CHRL) designation is preferred Minimum of four (4) years of work experience in accommodations, disability management, and return to work programs or related functions, preferable within a unionized environment and/or a post-secondary or public sector organization Sound Knowledge of HR policies and practices, collective agreements, and relevant employment legislation, such as the Employment Standards Act (ESA), Ontario Labour Relations Act (OLRA), Ontario Human Rights Code (OHRC), Occupational Health and Safety Act, and the Accessibility for Ontarians with Disabilities Act (AODA) Knowledge of both HR trends, and academic issues is preferred. Demonstrated approach that centers around employee wellbeing and legal responsibilities with a demonstrated commitment to the principles of equity, diversity, and inclusion A collaborative and consultative working style with an ability to develop and maintain effective working relationships in a manner that gains trust, respect and cooperation Excellent communication and interpersonal skills and judgement, with a demonstrated ability to deal with sensitive or confidential matters with discretion Qualities that include integrity, openness, fairness, resourcefulness Strong computer skills and a working knowledge of all Microsoft Office applications and well-developed internet research skills; knowledge of Human Resources Information Systems (HRIS) Demonstrated ability to work independently and participate collaboratively in a team environment This posting is for an existing vacancy. Mode of Work: Hybrid Compensation: Hiring Range - $42.81 to $49.08 per hour commensurate with experience, plus 4% vacation pay and 7% in lieu of benefits; Salary Range - $42.81 to $56.26 per hour. Hours of Work: Monday to Friday, 35 hours per week. This is a temporary contract with an expected duration of 6 months. Application Deadline: Interested applicants are invited to submit an updated * resume and cover letter ( PDF ) by selecting \"Apply Now\" below. The review of applications will begin Wednesday October 14 th and will continue until the position has been filled. * Please upload your resume and cover letter as one PDF . Please name your upload: FirstName_LastName-Job Code As an institution committed to art, design, digital media and related scholarship, OCAD University recognizes the profound and essential value that diversity brings to the creation, reception and circulation of creative practices and discourse. OCAD University understands that valuing diverse creative practices and forms of knowledge are essential to and enrich the institution’s core mission and vision as an art and design university with a local and global scope. As an employer committed to employment equity, we encourage applications from members of equity-deserving communities including women, racialized and Indigenous persons, persons with disabilities, and persons of all sexual orientations and gender identities/expressions. OCAD University is committed to providing an inclusive and barrier free experience to applicants with accessibility needs. Requests for accommodation can be made at any stage during the recruitment process. Please contact People & Culture for more information or refer to OCADU’s Accommodation in Employment Policy . All qualified persons are encouraged to apply; however, Canadians and permanent residents of Canada will be given priority. While we thank all candidates for their interest, only those short-listed will be contacted. Back Share Apply Now"
    },
    "https://tre.tbe.taleo.net/tre01/ats/careers/v2/viewRequisition?org=OCADU&cws=37&rid=3896": {
      "title": "Executive Assistant, Office of the Vice-President, Academic & Provost (VPAP)",
      "description": "Department: Office of the Vice-President, Academic & Provost\nExecutive Assistant, Office of the Vice-President, Academic & Provost (VPAP) | Job Code | 3896 | Department | Office of the Vice-President, Academic & Provost | Employment Type | Permanent\nNew Search Login Page Position Description Executive Assistant, Office of the Vice-President, Academic & Provost (VPAP) Job Code 3896 Department Office of the Vice-President, Academic & Provost Employment Type Permanent Apply Now OCAD University acknowledges the ancestral territories of the Mississaugas of the Credit, the Haudenosaunee, the Anishinaabeg and the Huron-Wendat, who are the original owners and custodians of the land on which we live, work and create OCAD University is seeking a seasoned and highly capable Executive Assistant to join the Office of the Vice-President, Academic & Provost (VPAP). This is a high-level executive support role for someone who can step into a busy office, quickly understand what matters, and take ownership. We are looking for an experienced EA who is proactive, confident, exceptionally organized, and comfortable working alongside a senior executive in a fast-paced and dynamic environment. The successful candidate will anticipate needs and take initiative to keep priorities moving forward. You know how to manage competing demands, exercise sound judgement, identify what requires attention, and ensure important work stays on track. You are comfortable following up, asking the right questions, and respectfully ensuring that information, decisions and action items move forward. Reporting to the Director, Academic Affairs & Senate Secretary, and functionally to the Vice-President, Academic & Provost, you will be a key partner in the effective operation of the VPAP Office. What You’ll Do At the centre of the role is the effective management of the VPAP’s time, information, priorities and commitments. Responsibilities include: Own and actively manage the VPAP’s complex calendar, anticipating scheduling needs, resolving conflicts, coordinating high-level internal and external meetings, and ensuring the VPAP is prepared for upcoming commitments. Manage and triage the VPAP’s email inbox, identifying urgent and high-priority matters, tracking deadlines and follow-ups, responding to inquiries as directed, and ensuring important items do not fall through the cracks. Anticipate what the VPAP needs to be successful, compiling briefing materials, agendas, minutes, reports, updates and relevant background information in advance of meetings and decisions. Identify and escalate priority issues and, working with the VPAP and Director, coordinate appropriate follow-up to keep matters moving toward resolution. Draft, edit and coordinate correspondence, communications, presentations, reports and other materials on behalf of the VPAP and the Office. Handle highly confidential and sensitive information with exceptional judgement and discretion, including materials related to budgets, grievances and labour relations. Build strong working relationships across the academic portfolio and throughout the University, ensuring effective communication and coordination between the VPAP Office and its many partners. Liaise confidently and professionally with senior administrators, faculty, staff, students, external stakeholders, government officials and members of the public on behalf of the VPAP. Serve as an initial point of contact for complex or sensitive matters brought to the VPAP Office, using sound judgement to determine the appropriate pathway for resolution. Provide administrative and coordination support to academic committees, ad hoc committees and working groups, including committees identified under the University's Memorandum of Agreement. Conduct research and support analysis related to academic initiatives, institutional priorities and special projects. Prepare, monitor and reconcile VPAP Office budgets and related financial activities, including invoices and purchasing card statements. Lead the coordination of academic events, including conferences, receptions, workshops and other special initiatives. Develop and maintain effective electronic and administrative systems that support an organized, responsive executive office. Coordinate travel and accommodation arrangements as required. Serve as backup to the Quality Assurance Officer & Associate Senate Secretary, as required. Take on other responsibilities that contribute to the effective operation of the VPAP Office. Who We’re Looking For You are an experienced Executive Assistant who is comfortable operating with a high degree of independence and accountability. You assess what is happening, determine what needs attention, and take initiative to move the work forward. You are likely to thrive in this role if you: Have significant experience supporting a senior executive in a complex, fast-paced environment. Are proactive and solutions-oriented, with a natural instinct to anticipate needs and stay ahead of priorities. Are confident taking ownership of an executive’s calendar, inbox, priorities and follow-ups. Are a committed team player who wants to be an active and equally contributing member of a small, highly collaborative team. You pitch in when priorities shift and care about the success of the Office as a whole. Are able to successfully manage a high-volume workload in an environment characterized by frequent interruptions, evolving priorities and tight deadlines. Can comfortably and diplomatically follow up with senior leaders, obtain the information you need, and keep commitments and timelines moving forward. Know when to act independently, when to consult, and when something needs to be escalated. Are exceptionally organized and able to create order when priorities are shifting quickly. Bring confidence, professionalism, sound judgement and discretion to your work, and take pride in being someone who keeps things moving, closes loops and gets things done. What You Bring A post-secondary degree or an equivalent combination of education and experience. At least four (4) years of Executive Assistant experience, including direct support to senior executives. Experience in post-secondary education is an asset. Advanced proficiency with Microsoft Office applications, particularly Outlook and Teams, and the ability to quickly learn new systems and technology. Strong writing, research and financial administration skills, including experience preparing executive-level communications and working with budgets. A demonstrated commitment to equity and diversity and to fostering a respectful work and learning environment. The flexibility to occasionally adjust your hours to support the needs of a busy executive office. This posting is for an existing vacancy. Mode of Work: On Campus This position is expected to work on campus four (4) days per week, with one (1) remote workday. On-campus requirements may increase based on the operational needs of the unit. Compensation: Hiring Range - $66,925.74 to $76,724.74 per annum, commensurate with experience, plus benefits; Salary Range - $66,925.74 to $87,958.44 per annum. Hours of Work : Monday to Friday, 35 hours per week. Application Deadline: Interested applicants are invited to submit an updated * resume and cover letter ( PDF )by selecting \"Apply Now\" below. Review of applications will begin immediately and continue until the position has been filled. * Please upload your resume and cover letter as one PDF . Please name your upload: FirstName_LastName-Job Code As an employer committed to employment equity, we encourage applications from members of equity-deserving communities including women, racialized and Indigenous persons, persons with disabilities, and persons of all sexual orientations and gender identities/expressions. OCAD University is committed to providing an inclusive and barrier free experience to applicants with accessibility needs. Requests for accommodation can be made at any stage during the recruitment process. Please contact People & Culture for more information or refer to OCADU’s Accommodation in Employment Policy All qualified persons are encouraged to apply; however, Canadians and permanent residents of Canada will be given priority. While we thank all candidates for their interest, only those short-listed will be contacted. Back Share Apply Now"
    },
    "https://tre.tbe.taleo.net/tre01/ats/careers/v2/viewRequisition?org=OCADU&cws=37&rid=3868": {
      "title": "Executive Director, Strategic Revenue Initiatives",
      "description": "Department: Office of the Vice-President, Finance & Administration\nExecutive Director, Strategic Revenue Initiatives | Job Code | 3868 | Department | Office of the Vice-President, Finance & Administration | Employment Type | Temporary/Contract\nNew Search Login Page Position Description Executive Director, Strategic Revenue Initiatives Job Code 3868 Department Office of the Vice-President, Finance & Administration Employment Type Temporary/Contract Apply Now OCAD University acknowledges the ancestral territories of the Mississaugas of the Credit, the Haudenosaunee, the Anishinaabeg and the Huron-Wendat, who are the original owners and custodians of the land on which we live, work and create OCAD University , Canada’s largest and oldest art and design university, is a vibrant community of bold, curious and compassionate artists, designers and scholars who are imagining and creating a joyful, equitable and sustainable world. The University is an internationally renowned hub for art, design, digital media, research, innovation and creativity, and arts administration. It embraces collaborative and interdisciplinary approaches to change-making through art, design-thinking curriculum and research, making OCAD U a local, regional, national and global leader in art and design. It is an exciting time to join the OCAD U community as the University is at a key stage in its institutional transformation, with the implementation of its Academic and Strategic Plan and guiding principles — driving positive impact; decolonization, indigenization and equity; environmental sustainability; emerging as a vibrant hub; student centered approaches; innovation in learning teaching and research; and financial stewardship. The Executive Director, Strategic Revenue Initiatives provides strategic leadership for the identification, development, and advancement of new revenue-generating opportunities that support OCAD University’s long-term financial sustainability, institutional priorities, brand and community values. Reporting to the Vice-President, Finance & Administration (VPFA), the Executive Director is responsible for developing and implementing a comprehensive revenue diversification strategy focused on expanding existing revenue streams, identifying emerging business opportunities, and establishing new commercial ventures, partnerships and enterprise initiatives. The role works collaboratively across the University, including, but not limited to: the School of Continuing Studies, Copy & Print Services, Merchandise & Design Product Sales and Event Sales. The role operates at a strategic level, assessing market opportunities, developing business cases driving business performance, and identifying new opportunities for revenue growth. The Executive Director serves as a central catalyst for revenue growth and business development across the institution. While the role does not assume day-to-day operational responsibility for existing units or override the authority of leaders currently accountable for the various portfolios, the Executive Director is expected to work alongside established unit leaders to enhance revenue generation, operational sustainability, market reach and institutional growth. Success in this role requires the ability to influence, align, and mobilize cross-functional teams within this shared accountability framework to advance integrated revenue growth strategies. This is one (1) year appointment with the possibility of renewal dependent on the metrics of success and growth. Within six (6) months of the term, the incumbent will be expected to develop and present a strategic plan for the University that allows for revenue growth within the associated departments listed above. Summary of Responsibilities: Strategic Leadership & Revenue Growth Develop and implement a comprehensive auxiliary revenue diversification strategy aligned with OCAD U’s strategic, academic, financial and operational priorities. Lead the development  of formal business plans, feasibility studies, financial models and implementation plans for new revenue initiatives. Identify and evaluate new revenue-generating opportunities, partnerships, and commercial ventures, including market expansion and innovation initiatives. Ensure auxiliary operations contribute meaningfully to institutional financial sustainability targets. Enterprise Growth Initiatives In collaboration with members of the Executive Team, lead cross-functional initiatives designed to generate new sources of institutional revenue Establish and monitor key performance indicators (KPIs), financial targets, and benchmarking measures aligned with sector best practices. Collaborate with leaders to identify new opportunities for professional learning, auxiliary services, facility use, and partnership development. Facilitate the exploration and development of new enterprises and revenue-generating services. Drive continuous improvement, innovation, and cost optimization across auxiliary functions. Support existing unit leaders in assessing opportunities for revenue expansion, while respecting their operational accountability and decision-making authority within their portfolios. Develop frameworks to help prioritize revenue opportunities based on strategic alignment, financial viability, market demand, operational capacity, risk and institutional impact. Financial Management & Business Planning Develop financial projections, business models, revenue forecasts, and return on investment analyses for proposed revenue initiatives. Ensure rigorous financial analysis, performance tracking, and reporting frameworks are in place to support decision-making. Monitor and evaluate the performance of approved revenue diversification initiatives against established targets, timelines and key performance indicators. Advise pricing strategies, contract negotiations, and commercial agreements to maximize revenue and mitigate risk in collaboration with unit heads to ensure new business initiatives align with the University’s long-term strategy Support integrated planning across revenue-generating activities to reduce duplication, identify synergies and align initiatives with institutional priorities. Ensure financial analysis and reporting frameworks support transparent, evidence-informed decision-making Governance, Reporting & Accountability Ensure strong governance, compliance, and risk management practices across auxiliary enterprises. Develops implementation plans for approved revenue initiatives and monitors execution progress, reporting regularly to the Vice-President, Finance & Administration on status, outcomes, risks, and corrective actions required Provide no less frequent than semi-annual updates to the Audit, Finance and Risk Committee (AFRC) on operations, revenue metrics, and KPI achievement. Prepare and present strategic reports and recommendations to senior leadership and governing bodies. Ensure transparency and alignment with institutional policies, regulatory requirements, and public-sector accountability expectations. Stakeholder Engagement & Partnerships Build and maintain strong relationships with internal stakeholders, including Deans, administrative leaders, and service units. Develop and manage external partnerships with vendors, service providers, and commercial collaborators. Represent the University in negotiations with external partners and business development initiatives. People Leadership Lead project teams, working groups, and cross-functional initiatives in support of revenue diversification objectives. Foster a culture of accountability, innovation, collaboration, and service excellence. Provide strategic guidance, business development expertise, and mentorship to colleagues engaged in revenue-generating initiatives. Support change management activities associated with the development and implementation of new revenue opportunities. Qualifications: A graduate degree in Business Administration, Commerce, Finance, or a related field, or an equivalent combination of education and experience with special consideration for the professional and community-based experience of equity-deserving groups Minimum ten (10) years of progressive leadership experience in revenue-generating operations, auxiliary services, commercial enterprises, or related fields. Experience within a post-secondary institution or similarly complex, public-sector environment is strongly preferred, with specific experience and knowledge of Continuing Studies in higher education. Demonstrated success in developing business strategies, growing revenue, and managing multi-unit operations. Demonstrated commercial revenue-development experience in a University, Continuing Studies, executive education, professional learning or comparable public-purpose institutions. Experience reporting to senior executives and governance bodies (e.g., Board committees). Strong business acumen, financial management, and strategic planning capabilities. Demonstrated ability to drive revenue growth while balancing mission and public accountability. Expertise in business case development, financial modeling, and scenario planning. Exceptional stakeholder management and influencing skills. Strong communication skills, including executive-level presentation and reporting. Ability to lead change, innovation, and complex organizational initiatives. Ability to both think and act strategically, balancing vision with the ability to implement that vision. A collaborative and consultative working style with an ability to develop and maintain effective working relationships internally and externally in a manner that gains & maintains trust, respect and cooperation. Excellent interpersonal skills and judgement, with a demonstrated ability to deal with sensitive or confidential matters with discretion. Personal qualities that include integrity, openness, fairness, resourcefulness, and confidence, coupled with an accessible style, optimism, enthusiasm and high emotional intelligence Demonstrated commitment to the principles of equity, diversity, inclusion, accessibility, decolonization and Indigenization. Mode of Work: Hybrid Compensation: $138,200.00 to $162,554.00 annually. * The successful incumbent has the option of pay in lieu or enrollment in benefits. Hours of Work: Monday to Friday, 35 hours per week. This is a temporary contract with an expected duration of 1 year. Application Deadline: Interested applicants are invited to submit an updated * resume and cover letter ( PDF ) by selecting \"Apply Now\" below. The review of applications will begin September 21 st 2026 and will continue until the position has been filled. * Please upload your resume and cover letter as one PDF . Please name your upload: FirstName_LastName-Job Code As an institution committed to art, design, digital media and related scholarship, OCAD University recognizes the profound and essential value that diversity brings to the creation, reception and circulation of creative practices and discourse. OCAD University understands that valuing diverse creative practices and forms of knowledge are essential to and enrich the institution’s core mission and vision as an art and design university with a local and global scope. As an employer committed to employment equity, we encourage applications from members of equity-deserving communities including women, racialized and Indigenous persons, persons with disabilities, and persons of all sexual orientations and gender identities/expressions. OCAD University is committed to providing an inclusive and barrier free experience to applicants with accessibility needs. Requests for accommodation can be made at any stage during the recruitment process. Please contact People & Culture for more information or refer to OCAD U’s Policy of Accommodation in Employment for Persons with Disabilities . All qualified persons are encouraged to apply; however, Canadians and permanent residents of Canada will be given priority. While we thank all candidates for their interest, only those short-listed will be contacted. Back Share Apply Now"
    },
    "https://tre.tbe.taleo.net/tre01/ats/careers/v2/viewRequisition?org=OCADU&cws=37&rid=3908": {
      "title": "Graduate Research Assistant: Alter-Pedagogies: Neurodivergent Research-Creation and Learning Otherwise",
      "description": "Department: Research Labs & Projects\nGraduate Research Assistant: Alter-Pedagogies: Neurodivergent Research-Creation and Learning Otherwise | Job Code | 3908 | Department | Research Labs & Projects | Employment Type | Temporary/Contract\nNew Search Login Page Position Description Graduate Research Assistant: Alter-Pedagogies: Neurodivergent Research-Creation and Learning Otherwise Job Code 3908 Department Research Labs & Projects Employment Type Temporary/Contract Apply Now OCAD University acknowledges the ancestral territories of the Mississaugas of the Credit, the Haudenosaunee, the Anishinaabeg and the Huron-Wendat, who are the original owners and custodians of the land on which we work, stand and create. OCAD University , Canada’s largest and oldest art and design university, is a vibrant community of bold, curious and compassionate artists, designers and scholars who are imagining and creating a joyful, equitable and sustainable world. The University is an internationally renowned hub for art, design, digital media, research, innovation and creativity, and arts administration. It embraces collaborative and interdisciplinary approaches to change-making through art, design-thinking curriculum and research, making OCAD U a local, regional, national and global leader in art and design. It is an exciting time to join the OCAD U community. The University is at a key stage in its institutional transformation, with the implementation of its Academic and Strategic Plan and guiding principles—driving positive impact, decolonization, indigenization, and equity, environmental sustainability, emerging as a vibrant hub, student-centered approaches, innovation in learning, teaching, and research, and financial stewardship. Project Details: Alter-Pedagogies is a SSHRC-funded Connections project that explores neurodivergent research-creation and approaches to teaching and learning that challenge conventional educational norms. The project will establish a Neurodivergent Research-Creation Community of Practice and support a series of workshops, public events and digital knowledge mobilization activities that bring together artists, educators, researchers and students. Drawing on disability studies, neurodiversity studies, accessibility, artistic research and research-creation, the project explores how neurodivergent ways of knowing, creating, learning and relating can contribute to new possibilities for teaching and learning. Project outputs will be curated and shared through an accessible digital platform designed to support ongoing dialogue and knowledge sharing within and beyond art and design education. Responsibilities Working under the supervision of the Principal Investigators, Dr. Maria-Belén Ordóñez and Dr. Laura Thrasher, the Graduate Research Assistant will support research activities, project administration and coordination, documentation, and knowledge mobilization. Compiling and curating literature reviews related to disability studies, neurodiversity studies, crip theory, accessibility, research-creation, and alter-pedagogies. Coordinating project activities, timelines, meetings, and communications, including maintaining project communications through Microsoft Teams and email and facilitating accessibility check-ins with participants and collaborators. Taking meeting notes and supporting project documentation, record keeping, and the organization of resources and materials generated through project activities. Supporting the planning and organization of workshops, Community of Practice gatherings, and public events. Assisting with evaluation activities, reporting, and other project administration tasks, including budget tracking and administration of research activities. Collaborating with the Principal Investigators, undergraduate student facilitators, and community partners throughout the project. Contributing to a safe and secure environment for all members of the University community and its facilities. Qualifications Current graduate student Strong organizational, administrative, and project coordination skills. Excellent written and verbal communication skills. Ability to work independently and collaboratively. Experience supporting events, workshops, research projects, or community-engaged initiatives. Demonstrated interest in accessibility, disability justice, neurodiversity, inclusive pedagogy, or related areas. Experience with Microsoft 365, Microsoft Teams, and shared digital workspaces. Demonstrated commitment to equity, diversity, and fostering respectful and inclusive work and learning environments. Applicants with lived experience of disability, neurodivergence, and other forms of embodied difference are encouraged to apply. Hours of Work: 8 hours per week over the period of November 1, 2026 to January 31, 2027, for a total of 80 hours. Work Environment: The position will be a hybrid arrangement and include both in-person and remote work. Compensation: $37.28 (Graduate Rate) per hour with an additional 4% in lieu of vacation and 6% in lieu of benefits Please note the following: Applicants must be eligible to work in Canada for any employer (with a valid work or study permit, SIN and Canadian bank account). Current students at OCAD University will be given priority consideration for all Research Assistant positions, with the highest priority given to those not currently holding any other TA/RA positions at the University. This appointment is conditional upon funding of the Project by OCAD U’s external funding partner(s) Application Process: Applications should include: A one-page cover letter explaining the candidate’s interest A curriculum vitae The contact information for two (2) referees Interested applicants are invited to submit their application in confidence addressed to Maria-Belén Ordóñez, Assistant Professor, Faculty of Arts and Science and Laura Thrasher, Educational Developer, Centre for Learning and Teaching lthrasher@ocadu.ca, online by clicking the “Apply for this Position” button below. The review of applications will begin on October 22, 2026 and will continue until the position has been filled. As an employer committed to employment equity, we encourage applications from members of equity-seeking communities including women, racialized and Indigenous persons, persons with disabilities, and persons of all sexual orientations and gender identities/expressions. We encourage members of designated equity-seeking groups to self-identify within the voluntary Applicant Questionnaire . OCAD U is committed to providing an inclusive and barrier free experience to applicants with accessibility needs. Requests for accommodation can be made at any stage during the recruitment process. Please contact People & Culture for more information or refer to OCAD U’s Policy of Accommodation in Employment for Persons with Disabilities . All qualified persons are encouraged to apply; however, Canadians and permanent residents of Canada will be given priority. While we thank all candidates for their interest, only those short-listed will be contacted. Back Share Apply Now"
    },
    "https://tre.tbe.taleo.net/tre01/ats/careers/v2/viewRequisition?org=OCADU&cws=37&rid=3918": {
      "title": "Health Promoter, Harm Reduction Project",
      "description": "Department: Student Wellness Centre\nHealth Promoter, Harm Reduction Project | Job Code | 3918 | Department | Student Wellness Centre | Employment Type | Temporary/Contract\nNew Search Login Page Position Description Health Promoter, Harm Reduction Project Job Code 3918 Department Student Wellness Centre Employment Type Temporary/Contract Apply Now OCAD University acknowledges the ancestral territories of the Mississaugas of the Credit, the Haudenosaunee, the Anishinaabeg and the Huron-Wendat, who are the original owners and custodians of the land on which we live, work and create OCAD University , Canada’s largest and oldest art and design university, is a vibrant community of bold, curious and compassionate artists, designers and scholars who are imagining and creating a joyful, equitable and sustainable world. The University is an internationally renowned hub for art, design, digital media, research, innovation and creativity, and arts administration. It embraces collaborative and interdisciplinary approaches to change-making through art, design-thinking curriculum and research, making OCAD U a local, regional, national and global leader in art and design. It is an exciting time to join the OCAD U community as the University is at a key stage in its institutional transformation, with the implementation of its Academic and Strategic Plan and guiding principles — driving positive impact; decolonization, indigenization and equity; environmental sustainability; emerging as a vibrant hub; student centered approaches; innovation in learning teaching and research; and financial stewardship. Reporting to the Manager, Harm Reduction Project, the Health Promoter is responsible for supporting the implementation, delivery, and evaluation of the Integrated Harm Reduction and Mental Health Capacity Building Initiative, funded by the Ministry of Colleges, Universities, Research Excellence and Security (MCURES). This multi-phase initiative is delivered in partnership with Toronto Metropolitan University, George Brown Polytechnic, Parkdale Queen West Community Health Centre, and The Neighbourhood Group Community Services. The Health Promoter will play a key role in advancing project objectives through the development and delivery of health promotion activities, harm reduction education, substance use literacy initiatives, overdose response training, student outreach, resource development, peer engagement, and knowledge dissemination. In addition to supporting the harm reduction initiative, this position contributes to the broader Student Wellbeing portfolio by promoting awareness of and access to student wellness and other student support offices. The Health Promoter will work collaboratively across Student Wellbeing teams and with community partners to develop and implement health promotion interventions that empower students to increase autonomy over their health and wellbeing. The incumbent will support the development of integrated care pathways, strengthen referral networks, build partnerships that enhance student support systems, and advocate for equitable access to services. This role contributes to fostering a culture of care grounded in trauma-informed, equity-based, anti-oppressive, health promotion, and harm reduction approaches. Summary of Responsibilities: Harm Reduction and Health Promotion, Training and Student Engagement Support the development, implementation, and evaluation of harm reduction, substance use literacy, mental health promotion, and overdose response initiatives. Co-facilitate workshops with other partner institutions, OCAD staff and students to lead training sessions, presentations, outreach activities, and awareness initiatives for students, staff, and faculty. Develop and implement strategies to increase awareness of harm reduction and mental health resources through digital, in-person, and drop-in programming. Conduct outreach during key periods including orientation, examinations, and other campus events, which may include evening and/or weekend. Respond to student inquiries and provide information, service navigation, and referrals to appropriate campus and community supports. Engage and provide day to day guidance to the Peer Health Educator in delivering programming and project deliverables Foster welcoming, inclusive, and non-judgmental spaces that encourage student engagement and help-seeking. Peer Engagement and Community Partnerships Collaborate with the project team, including cross-institutional counterparts, peers and student communities to co-design and deliver outreach initiatives, events, campaigns, and educational content. Support the training of peers, including topics such as harm reduction, communication skills, boundaries, confidentiality, de-escalation, and referral pathways. Assist with coordination of community partners participating in project activities and events. Build and maintain positive working relationships with students, community partners, and cross-institutional stakeholders to support project goals. Work with the Manager, Harm Reduction Project to further develop integrated care pathway models. Expand the reach of the Student Wellbeing portfolio by reaching out to create referral pathways for students Knowledge Dissemination and Project Implementation Develop and adapt educational materials, resource guides, toolkits, and communications to ensure accessibility, cultural responsiveness, and relevance to diverse student communities. Support creation of promotional materials and project communications. Assist with planning and coordination of the student-led podcast and related knowledge mobilization activities. Support student participation in content development and contribute to dissemination of project resources, findings, and educational materials across institutional channels. Participate in project planning, implementation, and delivery of project objectives and deliverables. Evaluation, Reporting and Administration Collect and maintain participation, engagement, and evaluation data. Support survey development, data collection, analysis, and reporting activities. Assist in preparing reports, presentations, and knowledge-sharing materials. Participate in continuous quality improvement activities and incorporate student and stakeholder feedback into programming. Participate in project, departmental, and team meetings as required. Perform other related duties that support the successful implementation of the project. Qualifications: Undergraduate degree in a related field (e.g., social work, social justice, public health, community development) and a minimum of three (3) years of direct experience providing community-based support in harm reduction, substance use and/or mental, and/or community development settings. Demonstrated commitment to harm reduction principles, with applied experience integrating a harm reduction lens in community-based and post-secondary environments. Direct experience working with individuals and communities affected by structural and systemic inequities, and a strong understanding of intersectionality and how social location shapes access to mental health, substance use, and health-related supports. Proven ability to support individuals with complex, intersectional needs, including navigating fragmented systems and addressing barriers related to housing, income, stigma, and health care access. Strong alignment with student-centred, peer-informed approaches, valuing lived and living experience and supporting co-creation and participatory engagement. Demonstrated ability to work collaboratively across multiple partners and stakeholders (e.g., post-secondary institutions, community organizations), with strong interpersonal, communication, and relationship-building skills. Experience designing, facilitating, and evaluating workshops, groups, education sessions, and/or outreach activities that are engaging, inclusive, and responsive to diverse communities. Experience supporting student engagement initiatives and peer-led programming, centering their voices and expertise Strong knowledge of the social determinants of health, with a demonstrated commitment to accessibility, inclusion, and health equity in program design and delivery. Excellent organizational skills, with a proven ability to manage competing priorities, maintain accuracy, and work effectively in a dynamic, multi-site project environment. Demonstrated judgment, tact, and discretion in handling confidential and sensitive information, with the ability to respond calmly and appropriately in crisis or urgent situations and seek consultation as needed. Ability to work independently with initiative and sound judgment, while also contributing effectively to a collaborative, cross-partner team. Experience responding to urgent or crisis situations, including overdose response, with appropriate training and adherence to protocols (e.g., consultation, documentation, escalation pathways). Current or willingness to obtain Standard First Aid, CPR, naloxone administration, and overdose response training. Demonstrated commitment to the principles of equity, diversity, inclusion, accessibility, and creating respectful learning and work environments. Strong computer literacy, including proficiency with Microsoft Office applications and ability to use digital tools for communication, reporting, and program delivery. Flexibility to work occasionally outside of standard business hours, including evenings and/or weekends, to support outreach and student engagement activities. Mode of Work: On Campus Compensation: Hiring Range - $31.59 to $36.14 per hour commensurate with experience, plus 4% vacation pay and 7% in lieu of *benefits; Salary Range - $31.59 to $41.52 per hour. *The successful incumbent has the option of pay in lieu or enrollment in benefits. Hours of Work: Monday to Friday, 35 hours per week. This is a temporary contract expected to continue until March 31, 2028 . Application Deadline: Interested applicants are invited to submit an updated * resume and cover letter ( PDF ) by selecting \"Apply Now\" below no later than Sunday November 8th 2026 . * Please upload your resume and cover letter as one PDF . Please name your upload: FirstName_LastName-Job Code As an institution committed to art, design, digital media and related scholarship, OCAD University recognizes the profound and essential value that diversity brings to the creation, reception and circulation of creative practices and discourse. OCAD University understands that valuing diverse creative practices and forms of knowledge are essential to and enrich the institution’s core mission and vision as an art and design university with a local and global scope. As an employer committed to employment equity, we encourage applications from members of equity-deserving communities including women, racialized and Indigenous persons, persons with disabilities, and persons of all sexual orientations and gender identities/expressions. OCAD University is committed to providing an inclusive and barrier free experience to applicants with accessibility needs. Requests for accommodation can be made at any stage during the recruitment process. Please contact People & Culture for more information or refer to OCADU’s Accommodation in Employment Policy . All qualified persons are encouraged to apply; however, Canadians and permanent residents of Canada will be given priority. While we thank all candidates for their interest, only those short-listed will be contacted. Back Share Apply Now"
    },
    "https://tre.tbe.taleo.net/tre01/ats/careers/v2/viewRequisition?org=OCADU&cws=37&rid=3901": {
      "title": "Indigenous Outreach & Recruitment Coordinator",
      "description": "Department: Indigenous Student Centre\nIndigenous Outreach & Recruitment Coordinator | Job Code | 3901 | Department | Indigenous Student Centre | Employment Type | Permanent\nNew Search Login Page Position Description Indigenous Outreach & Recruitment Coordinator Job Code 3901 Department Indigenous Student Centre Employment Type Permanent Apply Now OCAD University acknowledges the ancestral territories of the Mississaugas of the Credit, the Haudenosaunee, the Anishinaabeg and the Huron-Wendat, who are the original owners and custodians of the land on which we live, work and create OCAD University , Canada’s largest and oldest art and design university, is a vibrant community of bold, curious and compassionate artists, designers and scholars who are imagining and creating a joyful, equitable and sustainable world. The University is an internationally renowned hub for art, design, digital media, research, innovation and creativity, and arts administration. It embraces collaborative and interdisciplinary approaches to change-making through art, design-thinking curriculum and research, making OCAD U a local, regional, national and global leader in art and design. It is an exciting time to join the OCAD U community as the University is at a key stage in its institutional transformation, with the implementation of its Academic and Strategic Plan and guiding principles — driving positive impact; decolonization, indigenization and equity; environmental sustainability; emerging as a vibrant hub; student centered approaches; innovation in learning teaching and research; and financial stewardship. Reporting to the Manager, Indigenous Student Centre, the Indigenous Outreach & Recruitment Coordinator works to create pathways for Indigenous learners and their supporters/families to access, engage, and thrive within post-secondary education at OCAD University (OCAD U). The Coordinator plays a key role in supporting pathway navigation and coordinating culturally grounded engagement activities for Indigenous learners in their home communities Working in close partnership with the Coordinator, Indigenous Admissions & Recruitment Support, the Indigenous Outreach & Recruitment Coordinator leads outreach, recruitment, and prospective learner engagement initiatives in Indigenous communities outside the GTA, supporting learners from initial contact through the admissions process. While each Coordinator maintains primary responsibility for their respective portfolio, they work collaboratively to share information, coordinate referrals, support joint events and outreach initiatives, and provide reciprocal support as needed to ensure a coordinated and seamless experience for prospective Indigenous learners Summary of Responsibilities : Support Indigenous learners as they navigate pathways to OCAD U by meeting individually and in groups with prospective Indigenous learners and their supporters/families to provide support and advising and ensure access to relevant holistic and wraparound supports both on and off campus. In consultation with the Manager, Indigenous Student Centre, develop and coordinate culturally grounded transition programming for Indigenous learners with focus on pathway navigation, acclimation to the GTA, and connection to Indigenous community resources Work with Coordinator, Indigenous Admissions & Recruitment Support to conduct follow-up with prospective Indigenous students and/or their supporters/families; advocating for applicants and if needed, present special cases for consideration to support transition, engagement, retention, and overall student success. Knowledge of Indigenous Services Canada Post-Secondary Student Support Program to support Indigenous prospective learners in their Sponsorship applications, advise on process, liaise with Indigenous communities as needed, and coordinate with relevant internal support units to ensure smooth sponsorship and funding support for Indigenous students Collaborate with the Manager, Indigenous Student Centre to plan and coordinate participation in the Aboriginal Post-Secondary Information Program (APSIP) Tour; including extensive travel to secondary schools and Indigenous communities in Ontario Support the OCAD U tuition grant for Six Nations of the Grand River and Mississaugas of the Credit First Nation; acting as the primary contact for both communities, coordinating visits, supporting applicants through the enrollment process, and liaising with post-secondary education offices Develop, plan, and deliver off campus community-based initiatives and programming   with the intent of creating reciprocal relationships between OCAD University and Indigenouscommunities and organizations Strategize with the Manager, Indigenous Student Centre and the DirectorIndigenous Initiatives in the development and expansion of recruitment related partnerships with Indigenous communities outside of the GTA Maintain current knowledge of all OCAD U programs, admissions processes, supports, and student services; including an understanding of barriers and challenges faced by prospective Indigenous students, applicants, and admitted students Collaborate with the Indigenous Student Centre and the Coordinator, Indigenous Admissions & Recruitment Support to identify non-academic supports (e.g. financial aid, housing, childcare and community resources) to support Indigenous applicants as they transition to OCAD U’s downtown Toronto campus Identify and promote entrance awards, scholarships and bursaries available to Indigenous students Collaborate with the ISC and Indigenous Visual Culture Program to identify recruitment and outreach opportunities that support community awareness and engagement Manage the recruitment resources (budget and time) to ensure sustainable achievement of recruitment and outreach goals; ensuring compliance with OCAD U Travel, Meal and Hospitality Policy Support the evaluation of recruitment and outreach activities through data collection, documentation, and report writing Track and respond to applicant and community-based organizations and educator inquiries using the CRM database (Slate) Support the Manager, Indigenous Student Centre in other activities contributing to the successful operation of the Indigenous Student Centre Qualifications : Indigenous citizenship (First Nations, Inuit, Métis), connection to an Indigenous community, a history and/or evidence of lived experience and an understanding of Indigenous culture(s) and practices Knowledge of the issues Indigenous students face in relation to access, transition and retention from urban and rural environments to postsecondary education Understanding of Indigenous cultural, educational, political and economic conditions Three (3) years of administrative work experience, preferably in a post-secondary educational environment, or equivalent Two (2) years of experience working with diverse Indigenous communities through community outreach, engagement, programming and/or relationship-building. Post-secondary degree in Art or Design (concentration in Indigenous practice or history considered an asset); or an equivalent combination of education and experience Demonstrated ability to build and maintain reciprocal relationships with Indigenous communities, students, and families Experience with the administration of Indigenous community programming, education and arts Strong interpersonal, communication (both oral and written) and presentation skills; with the ability to work both independently and collaboratively in a team environment Strong organizational and analytical skills A demonstrated commitment to providing outstanding customer service Demonstrated commitment to the principles of equity and diversity, and experience promoting a respectful work and learning environment for students, staff and faculty Flexibility in work hours; must be willing and able to travel and work irregular hours (occasional evenings and weekends), as required A valid G class driver’s licence (no vehicle required) Mode of Work: On Campus Compensation: Hiring Range - $57,486.45 to $65,772.56 per annum, commensurate with experience, plus benefits; Salary Range - $57,486.45 to $75,566.13 per annum. Hours of Work : Monday to Friday, 35 hours per week. Application Deadline: Interested applicants are invited to submit an updated * resume and cover letter ( PDF )by selecting \"Apply Now\" below. Review of applications will begin November 9th and continue until the position is filled. * Please upload your resume and cover letter as one PDF . Please name your upload: FirstName_LastName-Job Code Application Details: The successful applicant will have Indigenous citizenship, connection to an Indigenous community (First Nations, Inuit, Métis) and will have a commitment to Indigenous community development, partnership building, and growing OCAD University’s priority to decolonize and Indigenize art and design education. Candidates are encouraged to share information in their cover letter about their Indigenous citizenship and relevant experience with Indigenous communities . If you are an Indigenous applicant and require support during the recruitment process, please reach out to Olivia Bowes, Talent Acquisition Coordinator at oliviabowes@ocadu.ca . As an employer committed to employment equity, we encourage applications from members of equity-deserving communities including women, racialized and Indigenous persons, persons with disabilities, and persons of all sexual orientations and gender identities/expressions. We encourage members of designated equity-deserving groups to self-identify within the voluntary Applicant Questionnaire. In order to alleviate the under-representation of Indigenous administrators, priority in hiring will be given to qualified Indigenous persons who identify as such in the application process. This initiative is a special program under the Ontario Human Rights Code. OCAD University is committed to providing an inclusive and barrier free experience to applicants with accessibility needs. Requests for accommodation can be made at any stage during the recruitment process. Please contact People & Culture for more information or refer to OCADU’s Accommodation in Employment Policy All qualified persons are encouraged to apply; however, Canadians and permanent residents of Canada will be given priority. While we thank all candidates for their interest, only those short-listed will be contacted. Back Share Apply Now"
    },
    "https://tre.tbe.taleo.net/tre01/ats/careers/v2/viewRequisition?org=OCADU&cws=37&rid=3836": {
      "title": "Indigenous Student Success Coordinator",
      "description": "Department: Indigenous Student Centre\nIndigenous Student Success Coordinator | Job Code | 3836 | Department | Indigenous Student Centre | Employment Type | Temporary/Contract\nNew Search Login Page Position Description Indigenous Student Success Coordinator Job Code 3836 Department Indigenous Student Centre Employment Type Temporary/Contract Apply Now OCAD University acknowledges the ancestral territories of the Mississaugas of the Credit, the Haudenosaunee, the Anishinaabeg and the Huron-Wendat, who are the original owners and custodians of the land on which we live, work and create OCAD University , Canada’s largest and oldest art and design university, is a vibrant community of bold, curious and compassionate artists, designers and scholars who are imagining and creating a joyful, equitable and sustainable world. The University is an internationally renowned hub for art, design, digital media, research, innovation and creativity, and arts administration. It embraces collaborative and interdisciplinary approaches to change-making through art, design-thinking curriculum and research, making OCAD U a local, regional, national and global leader in art and design. It is an exciting time to join the OCAD U community as the University is at a key stage in its institutional transformation, with the implementation of its Academic and Strategic Plan and guiding principles — driving positive impact; decolonization, indigenization and equity; environmental sustainability; emerging as a vibrant hub; student centered approaches; innovation in learning teaching and research; and financial stewardship. Reporting to the Manager, Indigenous Student Centre (ISC), the Indigenous Student Success Coordinator is responsible for providing Indigenous students with academic support, referrals to student support services both on and off campus, as well as enhancing Indigenous students’ experience through academic and cultural supports, programming, and activities. The Coordinator is responsive to individual student needs, providing support with financial aid, academic advising, and offering advocacy and guidance with academic and non-academic policies. The Coordinator is responsible for planning and implementing services and programs designed to facilitate the academic success of Indigenous students at OCAD University (OCAD U). The Coordinator works to ensure that Indigenous students are supported with culturally relevant services, working closely with Indigenous Student Centre staff, faculty and OCAD U support services to facilitate open, respectful communication to meet the needs of Indigenous students.  Regular on-site attendance is an essential requirement of this position to ensure accessible, responsive, and culturally appropriate support for students. Summary of Responsibilities: Responsible for coordinating, supporting, and enhancing core Indigenous Student Centre weekly programming and workshops aimed at supporting Indigenous students’ social, cultural and emotional wellbeing Collaborate with the Manager, Indigenous Student Centre and ISC team to create new programs that enhance the Indigenous student experience at OCAD University; support and facilitate the development of student-led programming in partnership with Indigenous students and student monitors. Maintain a consistent on-campus presence to provide support and referrals to Indigenous students in distress and as needed; act as a liaison between students and available supports Provide advocacy services and guidance to Indigenous students navigating academic and non-academic policies and processes such as academic misconduct, appeals, and petitions; formally advocate on behalf of Indigenous students and provide support through the relevant policy or process as needed or requested Provide academic advising, support and information to students pertaining to procedures, program requirements, dates and deadlines; work closely with Student Advising, Office of the Registrar and relevant support units Maintain current knowledge and comprehensive understanding of relevant academic and non-academic policies and processes including Student Petitions, Grade Appeals, Non-Academic Misconduct Policy, Response to Violent or Threatening Behaviour, Respectful Work and Learning Environment Policy; to effectively support student concerns and referrals to the relevant policy holder when necessary Maintain proactive communications, including the ISC weekly newsletter, social media accounts and the department’s website, to build awareness, promote events and ensure access to accurate, timely and relevant information; to connect with Indigenous OCAD U students, staff, faculty, alumni, Indigenous communities and partners Provide information and guidance to Indigenous students regarding internal and external bursaries, awards, financial supports; work closely with the Financial Aid & Awards office, providing referrals as needed Maintain knowledge of housing supports available for Indigenous OCAD U students; community programs, resources and housing partners Coordinate Orientation and Welcome events for new students and represent the Indigenous Student Centre at University events. With support from the Manager, Indigenous Student Centre, maintain collaborative relationships with OCAD U’s existing student support services to ensure Indigenous student needs are integrated into service delivery throughout the University; including Student Advising, Office of the Registrar, Student Wellness Centre, Student Accessibility Services, Centre for Learning and Teaching, Financial Aid & Awards, Centre for Emerging Artists & Designers Maintain a resource list for external agencies and organizations that provide supports for Indigenous students, including grants and awards, housing, career opportunities, cultural supports, and health and wellness Track persistence and retention of Indigenous students on an ongoing basis and develop retention plans in collaboration with the Manager, Indigenous Student Centre Supervise the Centre’s Student Monitors including participating in hiring, managing schedules, performance and approving biweekly time entry Serve on internal and external committees as needed Support the Manager, Indigenous Student Centre in other related functions and special projects contributing successful operation of the Indigenous Student Centre Qualifications: Indigenous citizenship (First Nations, Métis, Inuit), connection to an Indigenous community, lived experience and an understanding of the Indigenous culture(s) and practices Experience with the administration of Indigenous community programming, education, and arts Understanding of Indigenous cultural, educational, political, and economic conditions Post-secondary degree or equivalent, with three (3) years of administrative work experience, preferably in a post-secondary educational environment A demonstrated commitment to providing outstanding customer service in a diverse environment Excellent written and oral communication skills, interpersonal skills, and strong self-management skills Demonstrated professionalism, and the ability to exercise tact, good judgment and diplomacy Strong working knowledge of all Microsoft Office applications, and experience working with databases Demonstrated attention to detail, strong organizational and time management skills, and the ability to juggle competing demands, prioritize tasks and meet deadlines Demonstrated ability to take initiative and be an active contributor in the planning, formulation and implementation of student-centered services Proven ability to work independently and participate collaboratively in a team environment Demonstrated commitment to the principles of equity and diversity, and experience promoting a respectful work and learning environment for students, staff and faculty Flexibility in work hours; must be willing and able to work occasional evenings, as required Mode of Work: O n-campus 5 days per week Compensation: Hiring Range - $31.59 to $36.14 per hour commensurate with experience, plus 4% vacation pay and 7% in lieu of benefits; Salary Range - $31.59 to $41.52 per hour. Hours of Work: Monday to Friday, for a total of 35 hours per week. This is a temporary contract expected to continue until May 31, 2027. This posting is for an existing vacancy. Application Deadline: Interested applicants are invited to submit an updated * resume and cover letter ( PDF ) by selecting \"Apply Now\" below. The review of applications will begin August 7th, 2026 and will continue until the position has been filled. * Please upload your resume and cover letter as one PDF . Please name your upload: FirstName_LastName-Job Code As an institution committed to art, design, digital media and related scholarship, OCAD University recognizes the profound and essential value that diversity brings to the creation, reception and circulation of creative practices and discourse. OCAD University understands that valuing diverse creative practices and forms of knowledge are essential to and enrich the institution’s core mission and vision as an art and design university with a local and global scope. As an employer committed to employment equity, we encourage applications from members of equity-deserving communities including women, racialized and Indigenous persons, persons with disabilities, and persons of all sexual orientations and gender identities/expressions. OCAD University is committed to providing an inclusive and barrier free experience to applicants with accessibility needs. Requests for accommodation can be made at any stage during the recruitment process. Please contact People & Culture for more information or refer to OCAD U’s Policy of Accommodation in Employment for Persons with Disabilities . All qualified persons are encouraged to apply; however, Canadians and permanent residents of Canada will be given priority. While we thank all candidates for their interest, only those short-listed will be contacted. Back Share Apply Now"
    },
    "https://tre.tbe.taleo.net/tre01/ats/careers/v2/viewRequisition?org=OCADU&cws=37&rid=3917": {
      "title": "Knowledge Mobilization Coordinator",
      "description": "Department: Student Wellness Centre\nKnowledge Mobilization Coordinator | Job Code | 3917 | Department | Student Wellness Centre | Employment Type | Temporary/Contract\nNew Search Login Page Position Description Knowledge Mobilization Coordinator Job Code 3917 Department Student Wellness Centre Employment Type Temporary/Contract Apply Now OCAD University acknowledges the ancestral territories of the Mississaugas of the Credit, the Haudenosaunee, the Anishinaabeg and the Huron-Wendat, who are the original owners and custodians of the land on which we live, work and create OCAD University , Canada’s largest and oldest art and design university, is a vibrant community of bold, curious and compassionate artists, designers and scholars who are imagining and creating a joyful, equitable and sustainable world. The University is an internationally renowned hub for art, design, digital media, research, innovation and creativity, and arts administration. It embraces collaborative and interdisciplinary approaches to change-making through art, design-thinking curriculum and research, making OCAD U a local, regional, national and global leader in art and design. It is an exciting time to join the OCAD U community as the University is at a key stage in its institutional transformation, with the implementation of its Academic and Strategic Plan and guiding principles — driving positive impact; decolonization, indigenization and equity; environmental sustainability; emerging as a vibrant hub; student centered approaches; innovation in learning teaching and research; and financial stewardship. Reporting to the Manager, Harm Reduction Project, the Knowledge Translation and Content Coordinator is responsible for supporting project-wide communications, knowledge mobilization, content development, partnership engagement, and podcast production for the Integrated Harm Reduction and Mental Health Capacity Building Initiative, funded by the Ministry of Colleges, Universities, Research Excellence and Security (MCURES). Delivered in partnership with Toronto Metropolitan University (TMU), George Brown Polytechnic (GBP), Parkdale Queen West Community Health Centre, and The Neighbourhood Group Community Services, this multi-phase initiative aims to strengthen campus capacity through harm reduction training, community-partnered outreach, peer engagement, health promotion, and knowledge dissemination. The Knowledge Translation and Content Coordinator plays a key role in advancing project objectives by translating evidence-informed information into accessible, engaging, and student-centred formats. Working collaboratively with students, community partners, institutional stakeholders, and Student Wellbeing teams, the incumbent will support the development of educational resources, multimedia content, digital campaigns, and the student-led podcast. Through trauma-informed, equity-based, and stigma-reducing approaches, the position will contribute to increasing awareness, supporting community engagement, amplifying lived and living experience perspectives, and creating scalable resources for broader sector adoption. In addition to supporting the Harm Reduction Project, this position contributes to the broader Student Wellbeing portfolio by promoting awareness of and access to programs and services. The incumbent will develop partnerships, create knowledge-sharing opportunities, and support communications initiatives that connect students to the full spectrum of wellness supports available on campus and in the community. This position is expected to provide communications, content development, and knowledge translation support across Student Wellbeing initiatives as priorities require. Summary of Responsibilities: Knowledge Translation and Communications Lead development of project-wide communications and knowledge dissemination materials that support harm reduction, substance use literacy, overdose response education, mental health promotion, and stigma reduction. Translate research findings, evaluation data, project outcomes, and technical information into accessible, student-centered, plain-language resources. Develop educational materials including digital resources, toolkits, infographics, promotional materials, presentations, and knowledge-sharing products. Ensure all materials reflect harm reduction principles, trauma-informed practice, accessibility standards, anti-oppressive approaches, and cultural responsiveness. Support dissemination of project resources through institutional communication channels and partner networks. Contribute to the development of scalable resources and tools that can be adapted by other post-secondary institutions. Digital Engagement and Content Development Develop engaging multimedia content in support of project goals and activities. Create content for websites, social media platforms, newsletters, presentations, and awareness campaigns. Support promotion of training opportunities, harm reduction outreach activities, community events, and project initiatives across partner institutions. Maintain consistency in project branding, messaging, and visual identity. Monitor content performance and engagement metrics to inform continuous improvement efforts. Identify innovative approaches to student engagement through digital and multimedia platforms. Podcast Production and Knowledge Dissemination Coordinate planning, development, production, and promotion of the student-led podcast as a core knowledge mobilization initiative. Collaborate across campuses with students, the Harm Reduction Project teams, community partners, and subject matter experts to identify podcast themes, topics, and contributors, ensuring a range of perspectives. Support and facilitate recording sessions, interviews, and content development activities with students Develop production schedules, manage timelines, and coordinate workflows for podcast episodes and related multimedia content Edit and prepare podcast episodes and multimedia content for release across multiple platforms. Develop transcripts and accessible formats to maximize reach and accessibility. Ensure ethical and trauma-informed approaches to lived and living experience sharing. Collaborate with institutional media and communications channels across project partners to support dissemination, amplification, and sustainability of podcast content and related knowledge products Collaboration and Project Support Work closely with Harm Reduction project teams across participating institutions to identify emerging issues, student perspectives, and opportunities for knowledge dissemination. Collaborate with community partners and subject matter experts to develop evidence-informed content. Participate in project working groups, advisory committees, and planning meetings as required. Support project events, workshops, and outreach activities through content development, communications, documentation, and promotion. Assist with documenting project activities and capturing examples of impact, innovation, and promising practices. Evaluation and Reporting Support collection and organization of project stories, testimonials, engagement data, and knowledge dissemination metrics. Assist in development of reports, presentations, and publications related to project outcomes. Contribute to evaluation activities and knowledge mobilization efforts. Maintain organized archives of project materials, multimedia assets, resources, and communication products. Support final project deliverables and dissemination activities intended for broader sector use. Qualifications: Undergraduate degree in a field relevant to knowledge mobilization, communications, health or community-based work, or an equivalent combination of relevant education and experience. Minimum three (3) years of experience in communications, content development, health promotion, knowledge mobilization, digital engagement, podcast production, or related work. Demonstrated experience translating complex information into accessible and engaging content for diverse audiences. Experience developing digital content, educational resources, communications materials, or multimedia products. Strong understanding of storytelling approaches that engage audiences while maintaining accuracy, sensitivity, and ethical practice. Demonstrated commitment to harm reduction values and an appreciation for trauma-informed, anti-oppressive, equity-based, and culturally responsive approaches Direct experience or applied understanding of structural and systemic barriers impacting marginalized communities and the ways social location influences health and access to supports. Demonstrated commitment to reducing stigma related to substance use, mental health, and help-seeking. Experience supporting podcast production, audio/video content development, interviewing, editing, or related communications activities is an asset. Strong written communication skills, including editing, plain-language writing, and content development. Excellent interpersonal skills and ability to collaborate effectively with students, community partners, faculty, staff, and institutional stakeholders. Ability to engage respectfully and ethically with individuals sharing lived and living experience. Experience managing multiple projects, priorities, and timelines simultaneously. Proficiency with content development, digital communications, audio/video editing, and Microsoft Office applications. Demonstrated commitment to the principles of equity, diversity, inclusion, accessibility, and creating respectful learning and work environments. Ability to work independently with strong initiative, judgement, and attention to detail. Flexibility to work occasional evenings and weekends in support of project activities and events. Mode of Work: On Campus Compensation: Hiring Range - $31.59 to $36.14 per hour commensurate with experience, plus 4% vacation pay and 7% in lieu of *benefits; Salary Range - $31.59 to $41.52 per hour. * The successful incumbent has the option of pay in lieu or enrollment in benefits. Hours of Work: Monday to Friday, 35 hours per week. This is a temporary contract expected to continue until March 31, 2028. Application Deadline: Interested applicants are invited to submit an updated * resume and cover letter ( PDF ) by selecting \"Apply Now\" below no later than Sunday November 8th 2026 . * Please upload your resume and cover letter as one PDF . Please name your upload: FirstName_LastName-Job Code As an institution committed to art, design, digital media and related scholarship, OCAD University recognizes the profound and essential value that diversity brings to the creation, reception and circulation of creative practices and discourse. OCAD University understands that valuing diverse creative practices and forms of knowledge are essential to and enrich the institution’s core mission and vision as an art and design university with a local and global scope. As an employer committed to employment equity, we encourage applications from members of equity-deserving communities including women, racialized and Indigenous persons, persons with disabilities, and persons of all sexual orientations and gender identities/expressions. OCAD University is committed to providing an inclusive and barrier free experience to applicants with accessibility needs. Requests for accommodation can be made at any stage during the recruitment process. Please contact People & Culture for more information or refer to OCADU’s Accommodation in Employment Policy . All qualified persons are encouraged to apply; however, Canadians and permanent residents of Canada will be given priority. While we thank all candidates for their interest, only those short-listed will be contacted. Back Share Apply Now"
    },
    "https://tre.tbe.taleo.net/tre01/ats/careers/v2/viewRequisition?org=OCADU&cws=37&rid=3864": {
      "title": "Lead, Alumni Engagement",
      "description": "Department: Advancement\nLead, Alumni Engagement | Job Code | 3864 | Department | Advancement | Employment Type | Temporary/Contract\nNew Search Login Page Position Description Lead, Alumni Engagement Job Code 3864 Department Advancement Employment Type Temporary/Contract Apply Now OCAD University acknowledges the ancestral territories of the Mississaugas of the Credit, the Haudenosaunee, the Anishinaabeg and the Huron-Wendat, who are the original owners and custodians of the land on which we live, work and create OCAD University , Canada’s largest and oldest art and design university, is a vibrant community of bold, curious and compassionate artists, designers and scholars who are imagining and creating a joyful, equitable and sustainable world. The University is an internationally renowned hub for art, design, digital media, research, innovation and creativity, and arts administration. It embraces collaborative and interdisciplinary approaches to change-making through art, design-thinking curriculum and research, making OCAD U a local, regional, national and global leader in art and design. It is an exciting time to join the OCAD U community as the University is at a key stage in its institutional transformation, with the implementation of its Academic and Strategic Plan and guiding principles — driving positive impact; decolonization, indigenization and equity; environmental sustainability; emerging as a vibrant hub; student centered approaches; innovation in learning teaching and research; and financial stewardship. Reporting to the Director of Annual Giving & Alumni Relations and working closely with academic faculties and programs, Marketing & Communications, the RBC Centre for Emerging Artists & Designers, Admissions & Recruitment, Advancement, and other campus partners, the Lead, Alumni Engagement will assess, structure, and expand ways for alumni to be involved, creating a comprehensive roster of engagement opportunities that leverage alumni expertise and strengthen connections between alumni, students, faculty, and the broader OCAD U community. This role will identify and assess existing alumni engagement activities across the institution, establish processes that support coordination and collaboration, and develop new initiatives to address gaps and broaden opportunities for meaningful alumni participation. The position will contribute to building a more connected and engaged alumni community while supporting institutional priorities related to student success, experiential learning, mentorship, recruitment, and lifelong engagement. OCAD University is seeking a Lead, Alumni Engagement to help strengthen and coordinate alumni engagement across the institution. This one-year term position will play a critical role in creating a more centralized and strategic approach to alumni engagement. Summary of Responsibilities: Serve as the University's central point of contact for alumni engagement activities, providing coordination, guidance, and support across faculties, departments, and administrative units. Conduct an inventory of existing alumni engagement initiatives and develop and maintain a centralized roster of opportunities for alumni involvement. Create and implement an annual alumni engagement plan that supports institutional priorities and advances a coordinated, university-wide approach to alumni relations. Collaborate with academic faculties and departments to support alumni engagement objectives and strengthen connections between alumni and academic programs. Partner with the RBC Centre for Emerging Artists & Designers to expand opportunities for alumni involvement in mentorship, experiential learning, career development, and other student-facing initiatives. Work with Recruitment & Admissions to develop meaningful opportunities for alumni participation in student recruitment activities, including ambassador programs, portfolio reviews, outreach initiatives, and prospective student engagement. Identify gaps in existing programming and develop new initiatives that enhance alumni participation and strengthen connections among alumni, students, faculty, and OCAD U. Collaborate with Advancement and Marketing & Communications to promote alumni programs and opportunities through a variety of communication channels. Establish processes for tracking alumni engagement, maintain accurate records in Raiser's Edge and other systems, and monitor participation and outcomes. Engage with alumni to understand their interests, identify opportunities for involvement, and assess their willingness to participate in alumni programs and initiatives. Represent the Alumni Relations team at events by welcoming attendees and fostering meaningful connections with alumni. Evaluate the effectiveness of alumni engagement activities and provide recommendations for continuous improvement and long-term sustainability. Qualifications: Minimum of five (5) years of experience in alumni relations, community engagement, or stakeholder relations Post secondary degree, or equivalent Demonstrated ability to build and maintain effective relationships with a wide range of stakeholders, including alumni, students, faculty, staff, volunteers, and external partners. Experience developing, coordinating, and implementing engagement programs, events, or volunteer initiatives. Strong project management and organizational skills, with the ability to manage multiple priorities and meet deadlines in a collaborative environment. Excellent written, verbal, and interpersonal communication skills, with the ability to tailor messaging to diverse audiences. Strong analytical skills and experience collecting, tracking, and interpreting participation and engagement data to inform decision-making and evaluate outcomes. Experience working with constituent relationship management (CRM) systems; familiarity with Raiser's Edge or similar advancement databases is considered an asset. Demonstrated ability to work independently and collaboratively across departments and organizational levels. Knowledge of higher education, alumni relations, advancement, student engagement, or the arts and design sector is an asset. Demonstrated commitment to the principles of equity and diversity, and experience promoting a respectful work and learning environment for students, staff and faculty Mode of Work: Hybrid Compensation: $72,077.00 annually to $84,784.00 annually. * The successful incumbent has the option of pay in lieu or enrollment in benefits. Hours of Work: Monday to Friday, 35 hours per week. This is a temporary contract with an expected duration of 1 year. Application Deadline: Interested applicants are invited to submit an updated * resume and cover letter ( PDF ) by selecting \"Apply Now\" below. The review of applications will begin immediately and will continue until the position has been filled. * Please upload your resume and cover letter as one PDF . Please name your upload: FirstName_LastName-Job Code As an institution committed to art, design, digital media and related scholarship, OCAD University recognizes the profound and essential value that diversity brings to the creation, reception and circulation of creative practices and discourse. OCAD University understands that valuing diverse creative practices and forms of knowledge are essential to and enrich the institution’s core mission and vision as an art and design university with a local and global scope. As an employer committed to employment equity, we encourage applications from members of equity-deserving communities including women, racialized and Indigenous persons, persons with disabilities, and persons of all sexual orientations and gender identities/expressions. OCAD University is committed to providing an inclusive and barrier free experience to applicants with accessibility needs. Requests for accommodation can be made at any stage during the recruitment process. Please contact People & Culture for more information or refer to OCAD U’s Policy of Accommodation in Employment for Persons with Disabilities . All qualified persons are encouraged to apply; however, Canadians and permanent residents of Canada will be given priority. While we thank all candidates for their interest, only those short-listed will be contacted. Back Share Apply Now"
    },
    "https://tre.tbe.taleo.net/tre01/ats/careers/v2/viewRequisition?org=OCADU&cws=37&rid=3890": {
      "title": "Learning Skills Specialist",
      "description": "Department: Centre for Learning and Teaching\nLearning Skills Specialist | Job Code | 3890 | Department | Centre for Learning and Teaching | Employment Type | Temporary/Contract\nNew Search Login Page Position Description Learning Skills Specialist Job Code 3890 Department Centre for Learning and Teaching Employment Type Temporary/Contract Apply Now OCAD University acknowledges the ancestral territories of the Mississaugas of the Credit, the Haudenosaunee, the Anishinaabeg and the Huron-Wendat, who are the original owners and custodians of the land on which we live, work and create OCAD University , Canada’s largest and oldest art and design university, is a vibrant community of bold, curious and compassionate artists, designers and scholars who are imagining and creating a joyful, equitable and sustainable world. The University is an internationally renowned hub for art, design, digital media, research, innovation and creativity, and arts administration. It embraces collaborative and interdisciplinary approaches to change-making through art, design-thinking curriculum and research, making OCAD U a local, regional, national and global leader in art and design. It is an exciting time to join the OCAD U community as the University is at a key stage in its institutional transformation, with the implementation of its Academic and Strategic Plan and guiding principles — driving positive impact; decolonization, indigenization and equity; environmental sustainability; emerging as a vibrant hub; student centered approaches; innovation in learning teaching and research; and financial stewardship. Reporting to the Manager, Student Writing, Communication and Learning Support, the Learning Skills Specialist develops, plans and delivers learning support programs for undergraduate and graduate students at OCAD University (OCAD U) to support their academic success and promote their engagement with scholarly, creative and disciplinary discourses and communities with an emphasis on reducing barriers to learning for disabled students, including those registered with Student Accessibility Services (SAS). The Learning Skills Specialist collaborates with program staff to build capacity for learning support across Centre programs and service areas and works closely with SAS staff to facilitate holistic student supports, contributing to initiatives that address the intersectional learning experiences and enhance access to learning for OCAD U’s diverse student body. This role broadly serves as a resource in the areas of student learning skills development and accessible student-centred pedagogy, informed by a commitment to decolonizing, anti-racist approaches and an understanding of Disability Studies theory and practice. The Learning Skills Specialist works within the Centre for Learning & Teaching (CLT) as part of a larger team delivering a range of programs and services for faculty, students, Teaching Assistants and other instructional staff. Together, they collaborate with the university community to provide leadership and support that creates and sustains equitable and engaging teaching and learning environments for all OCAD University students. This position is eligible for hybrid work arrangements; however, a minimum of three days per week of on campus work is required with flexibility to adjust the days and times of work on campus to meet programming needs. Summary of Responsibilities: Participate in portfolio-wide program planning processes with reference to emerging student needs, curricular changes and OCAD University’s Academic and Strategic Plan Develop and deliver learning support programs (e.g. one-on-one, small group, peer-based, co-curricular, classroom-based) to facilitate student learning skills development with an emphasis on supporting the success of disabled students; consult with SAS staff and other stakeholders to identify ongoing and emerging student needs and to inform program development and delivery priorities Provide individual learning skills consultations for students drawing from learning strategy best practice Develop and provide student support, resources and learning opportunities in the use of learning technologies and applications for navigating and reducing barriers to learning and enhancing academic success Develop and deliver learning skills training and resources for program staff as part of annual pre-service and in-service tutor education to build capacity for learning skills support across program and service areas In consultation with the Lead, Co-Curricular and Peer Learning Programs, develop and deliver student learning skills-building workshops as part of student transition, orientation and retention programming with an emphasis on supporting the success of disabled students; collaborate with SAS to develop and deliver academic orientation and transition programming for students with disabilities Collaborate with the Lead, Co-curricular and Peer Learning Programs to develop and deliver academic skills workshops for students on probation Work closely with SAS to deliver student programming and faculty supports, as required, that strengthens access and accommodations processes for disabled students, including students registered with SAS Support the delivery of undergraduate academic integrity consultations and other resources as required; complete related reporting requirements Develop and maintain student learning resources to support their success in in person, blended and online learning environments and across all course types (e.g. studio, seminar, lecture) In consultation with the Lead, Co-Curricular and Peer Learning Programs, contribute to the development and implementation of student learning support program policies and procedures Facilitate timely and effective student referrals within the Centre and between the Centre, SAS and other areas of the university Develop web content as required and contribute to promotional and outreach initiatives related to learning skills programs and services Undertake research to identify promising practices and provide expertise and guidance across the staff team and the University to support the integration of evidence-based practice; contribute to faculty and staff professional learning opportunities to promote best practices in student learning skills development and accessible  pedagogy, informed by equity-based and Disability Studies approaches to education, as required Contribute to the development and implementation of program monitoring and evaluation tools and participate in critically reflective practice and evidence-informed program development and decision-making Actively contribute to Learning Skills professional communities and conversations as well as best practice investigations and advancements in learning skills development and accessible pedagogy for art and design education; share research findings through a variety of knowledge dissemination activities Participate in reflective practice and professional learning activities, individually and in teams, to integrate equity and sustainability principles across relevant programs, processes and practices Fulfill Centre and Ministry reporting requirements Participate in departmental planning in support of the university’s strategic and academic plans and Centre goals and objectives; participate on relevant permanent or ad-hoc University committees, as required Assist the Manager and Director in other related tasks that contribute to the successful operation of the Centre and support the Centre’s strategic directions Qualifications: Master’s degree in Education, Social Sciences or equivalent; Supplemental Instruction, Learning Disability Specialist or Learning Strategist training certificate an asset A minimum of five (5) years of experience providing learning and academic skills building programs to diverse learners and students with disabilities in a post-secondary environment; progressive experience working in a university academic success centre or equivalent In-depth understanding of the learning and academic support needs of diverse learners and students with disabilities Post-secondary studies in Art or Design an asset; past or present art or design practice an asset Strong knowledge of best practices in student learning theory and practice; demonstrated experience developing and implementing co-curricular peer learning programming Informed understanding of the opportunities, limitations and risks associated with artificial intelligence (AI) for student learning and academic success; active engagement with evolving research, pedagogical practices and sector developments related to AI use in learning skills education and support. Demonstrated understanding of critical pedagogy, equity studies, accessible pedagogy and Universal Design for Learning (UDL); understanding of Disability Studies theory and practices with experience translating Disability Studies approaches into student program development and program delivery practices Excellent program development skills with experience conducting needs assessments and stakeholder engagement to inform multi-year program planning Demonstrated experience designing, delivering and evaluating skills-building workshops for students; excellent group facilitation and instructional skills Demonstrated capacity for and experience in providing assistance to students in app and learning technology use for learning and academic success; experience giving students support in the use of assistive technology, an asset Excellent communication, interpersonal and leadership skills, and a demonstrated ability to exercise tact, sound judgment and commitment to confidentiality Excellent academic and professional writing skills with the ability to write effectively for different audiences Demonstrated experience producing educational resources and handouts including experience developing learning resources to support online learning environments Knowledge of best practices in multilingual pedagogy and an understanding of the barriers and support needs of post-secondary students studying in a second language Track record of working consultatively with faculty to build relationships and support student program development and delivery; experience developing and delivering faculty and TA professional learning programming an asset Demonstrated experience developing and integrating decolonizing, anti-racist, accessible and translingual approaches and pedagogies in previous work or study Demonstrated commitment to the principles of equity, anti-racism, decolonization and accessibility; and experience promoting a respectful work and learning environment for students, staff and faculty Demonstrated ability to take initiative, excellent organizational and time management skills Strong computer skills with knowledge of Microsoft 365 applications; knowledge of Canvas learning management system and Adobe Creative Cloud applications an asset Flexibility to adjust hours and location of work as needed to support programs and events outside of regular office hours and location This posting is for an existing vacancy. Mode of Work: Hybrid. A minimum of three days per week of on campus work is required with flexibility to adjust the days and times of work on campus to meet programming needs. Compensation: Hiring Range - $42.81 to $49.08 per hour commensurate with experience, plus 4% vacation pay and 7% in lieu of *benefits; Salary Range - $42.81 to $56.26 per hour. * The successful incumbent has the option of pay in lieu or enrollment in benefits. Hours of Work: Monday to Friday, for a total of 35 hours per week. This is a temporary contract expected to continue until June 5 th , 2028. Application Deadline: Interested applicants are invited to submit an updated * resume and cover letter ( PDF ) by selecting \"Apply Now\" below. Review of applications will begin on October 1, 2026 and continue until the position is filled * Please upload your resume and cover letter as one PDF . Please name your upload: FirstName_LastName-Job Code As an institution committed to art, design, digital media and related scholarship, OCAD University recognizes the profound and essential value that diversity brings to the creation, reception and circulation of creative practices and discourse. OCAD University understands that valuing diverse creative practices and forms of knowledge are essential to and enrich the institution’s core mission and vision as an art and design university with a local and global scope. As an employer committed to employment equity, we encourage applications from members of equity-deserving communities including women, racialized and Indigenous persons, persons with disabilities, and persons of all sexual orientations and gender identities/expressions. OCAD University is committed to providing an inclusive and barrier free experience to applicants with accessibility needs. Requests for accommodation can be made at any stage during the recruitment process. Please contact People & Culture for more information or refer to OCAD U’s Policy of Accommodation in Employment for Persons with Disabilities . All qualified persons are encouraged to apply; however, Canadians and permanent residents of Canada will be given priority. While we thank all candidates for their interest, only those short-listed will be contacted. Back Share Apply Now"
